1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
The 1970s, a time of great engineering feats.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
The CN Tower, the Sydney Opera House, and the Sears Tower.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:13,000
But also a time for engineering disasters.

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:17,000
An apartment complex under construction collapses.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
The Edmund Fitzgerald disappears into Lake Superior.

6
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:24,000
A dam breaks, wiping out an entire community.

7
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:28,000
And 14 perish when an arena self-destructs.

8
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:32,000
What were the flaws and reasons behind such destruction?

9
00:00:32,001 --> 00:00:36,000
Engineering disasters of the 70s, now on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,000
December 17th, 1976.

11
00:00:51,001 --> 00:00:54,000
A quiet evening at the Port of Los Angeles in San Pedro, California,

12
00:00:54,001 --> 00:00:59,000
was about to be shattered by an unexpected disaster.

13
00:00:59,001 --> 00:01:02,000
The Sansanina, a Liberian oil tanker,

14
00:01:02,001 --> 00:01:05,500
had just unloaded a cargo of 212,000 barrels

15
00:01:05,501 --> 00:01:07,500
of Indonesian crude.

16
00:01:07,501 --> 00:01:10,500
Her Italian crew then filled the empty cargo tanks

17
00:01:10,501 --> 00:01:12,500
with seawater.

18
00:01:12,501 --> 00:01:15,500
This is a routine practice called ballasting.

19
00:01:15,501 --> 00:01:17,500
It keeps the tanker riding low in the water

20
00:01:17,501 --> 00:01:20,500
on her voyage back at sea.

21
00:01:20,501 --> 00:01:25,500
About 7.35, somewhere along there in the evening,

22
00:01:25,501 --> 00:01:31,000
as I looked out that window, a tremendous pressure wave hit us.

23
00:01:31,001 --> 00:01:33,000
I thought somebody dropped the bomb.

24
00:01:33,001 --> 00:01:35,000
It sounded just like I looked out the window,

25
00:01:35,001 --> 00:01:37,000
and a big mushroom went up in the air.

26
00:01:37,001 --> 00:01:39,500
And I grabbed the fire phone, but it was dead.

27
00:01:39,501 --> 00:01:42,000
Everything had been cut off.

28
00:01:42,001 --> 00:01:46,000
The force of the blast could be felt 40 miles away.

29
00:01:46,001 --> 00:01:50,000
In response, some 200 firefighters raced to the pier.

30
00:01:50,001 --> 00:01:52,840
The first thing we saw when we arrived on the scene

31
00:01:52,940 --> 00:01:56,040
was a large mass of fire involving the wharf

32
00:01:56,140 --> 00:01:58,240
to such an extent that you couldn't identify

33
00:01:58,340 --> 00:02:00,140
what actually was out there.

34
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:02,940
It appeared to me that there was a large building there

35
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:06,380
on the wharf that was on fire, and yet I knew that there was

36
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:09,280
no building there of that size.

37
00:02:09,380 --> 00:02:11,460
The building Dahlquist thought he saw

38
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:14,720
was a chunk of the 70,000-ton San Sanina.

39
00:02:14,820 --> 00:02:17,360
Part of its midsection had blown out of the water

40
00:02:17,361 --> 00:02:19,700
and landed in the parking lot.

41
00:02:19,701 --> 00:02:22,700
The rest lay submerged at the bottom of the harbor.

42
00:02:22,701 --> 00:02:24,700
There was fire on top of the water.

43
00:02:24,701 --> 00:02:26,360
There was fire on the wharf.

44
00:02:26,361 --> 00:02:27,900
There was fire on the land.

45
00:02:27,901 --> 00:02:30,600
We simply worked all of those areas with our monitors

46
00:02:30,601 --> 00:02:34,520
until we were able to extinguish the fire.

47
00:02:34,521 --> 00:02:37,520
Remarkably, most of the 30 men aboard the Sansonina

48
00:02:37,521 --> 00:02:40,820
survived the initial blast, but they were still trapped

49
00:02:40,821 --> 00:02:43,520
in the ship's burning remains.

50
00:02:43,521 --> 00:02:46,120
Firemen like Walter Ball risked their own lives

51
00:02:46,121 --> 00:02:48,420
maneuvering a fireboat through the flaming debris

52
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:50,120
to reach the men.

53
00:02:50,220 --> 00:02:51,620
About the first five, all of them

54
00:02:51,720 --> 00:02:53,720
they wanted to do was go, go, let's get out of here.

55
00:02:53,820 --> 00:02:55,920
They thought it was going to explode again,

56
00:02:56,020 --> 00:02:58,560
because it was making all kinds of noises in the ship.

57
00:02:58,660 --> 00:03:01,160
But Ball would not move until the last of the 18 men

58
00:03:01,260 --> 00:03:05,880
he had found clambered down from the ship's stern.

59
00:03:05,980 --> 00:03:08,980
In the end, more than 50 people were injured

60
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:12,680
and nine people were dead, including three men whose bodies

61
00:03:12,780 --> 00:03:15,380
were never found.

62
00:03:15,381 --> 00:03:17,420
The cause of the blast that claimed their lives

63
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:20,620
and wrought so much destruction was a mystery.

64
00:03:20,720 --> 00:03:25,320
Accidents are generally caused by a series of small items

65
00:03:25,420 --> 00:03:28,020
which fall into alignment.

66
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:30,720
And it appeared that's what happened on the Sansonina

67
00:03:30,820 --> 00:03:31,820
that day.

68
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:33,440
The Coast Guard immediately launched

69
00:03:33,540 --> 00:03:36,140
an investigation into what caused the 18-year-old,

70
00:03:36,240 --> 00:03:40,540
70,600-ton ship to explode.

71
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:43,040
They focused on what the Sansonina crew was doing

72
00:03:43,041 --> 00:03:47,040
prior to the disaster, ballasting.

73
00:03:47,140 --> 00:03:50,040
Depositing seawater into the same tanks that just unloaded

74
00:03:50,140 --> 00:03:53,280
oil could have created combustible fumes.

75
00:03:53,380 --> 00:03:55,780
Well, in a tank, if you start to fill the water,

76
00:03:55,880 --> 00:03:57,680
of course, the water goes to the bottom of the tank.

77
00:03:57,780 --> 00:04:00,900
And what it does is it forces the fumes out the top of the tank.

78
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:04,200
Most hydrocarbon fumes, like gasoline fumes,

79
00:04:04,300 --> 00:04:05,500
are heavier than air.

80
00:04:05,600 --> 00:04:07,440
So what that means is when they get up,

81
00:04:07,540 --> 00:04:10,300
they don't readily dissipate up into the air.

82
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:12,500
It sits there until it's driven off

83
00:04:12,501 --> 00:04:14,000
or something of that nature.

84
00:04:16,500 --> 00:04:18,000
But on the day of the explosion,

85
00:04:18,100 --> 00:04:21,300
there was no wind, and the highly flammable vapor cloud

86
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:25,140
was trapped between the midship deckhouse and the aft castle.

87
00:04:25,240 --> 00:04:28,580
Well, if you have a huge volume of hydrocarbon mixed with air

88
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:34,160
and you ignite this, what you have created in reality

89
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:37,300
is what we call now a fuel air bomb.

90
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:42,500
And the effect, of course, can be devastating.

91
00:04:42,501 --> 00:04:44,500
What caused the vapor cloud to ignite

92
00:04:44,501 --> 00:04:46,340
will perhaps never be known.

93
00:04:46,341 --> 00:04:49,040
There were too many potential sources.

94
00:04:49,041 --> 00:04:52,580
Once you have hydrocarbon gas in the right ratio

95
00:04:52,581 --> 00:04:54,920
of fuel and air to be ignited, it

96
00:04:54,921 --> 00:04:58,120
will find an ignition source sooner or later.

97
00:04:58,121 --> 00:04:59,620
Trust me on an industrial system,

98
00:04:59,621 --> 00:05:01,380
there'll be an electric spark.

99
00:05:01,381 --> 00:05:03,460
There'll be something being hot.

100
00:05:03,461 --> 00:05:08,120
And it doesn't take much energy to kick this off.

101
00:05:08,121 --> 00:05:10,120
It isn't just what collects on deck.

102
00:05:10,121 --> 00:05:12,960
That's what causes and finds a source of ignition.

103
00:05:13,060 --> 00:05:16,460
There were conduction paths of air fuel mixtures

104
00:05:16,560 --> 00:05:19,460
down into the tanks.

105
00:05:19,560 --> 00:05:22,300
The safety and ventilation systems on the San San

106
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:24,360
Sanina to prevent fire from reaching the cargo tanks

107
00:05:24,460 --> 00:05:25,960
were ineffective.

108
00:05:26,060 --> 00:05:30,080
The flame screens are intended to prevent any flame

109
00:05:30,180 --> 00:05:33,680
from going into the cargo tanks on the ship.

110
00:05:33,780 --> 00:05:35,580
That was an industry practice at the time

111
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:38,280
and appears they were not in place.

112
00:05:38,281 --> 00:05:40,780
It also appears from the Coast Guard reports

113
00:05:40,880 --> 00:05:43,280
that some of the gas lines which were intended

114
00:05:43,380 --> 00:05:49,320
to carry gas vapors away to safer areas may have been rusted.

115
00:05:49,420 --> 00:05:53,020
Had the vents not been so rusted,

116
00:05:53,120 --> 00:05:54,520
they might have had a flash fire on deck,

117
00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:57,540
but they may not have had the big explosion of the ship.

118
00:05:57,640 --> 00:06:02,340
But all these things came together that day wrong,

119
00:06:02,440 --> 00:06:05,340
so a number of people died.

120
00:06:05,440 --> 00:06:08,040
In 1977, almost a year

121
00:06:08,041 --> 00:06:10,340
a year after the accident, the Coast Guard

122
00:06:10,341 --> 00:06:13,800
completed their investigation and issued a report.

123
00:06:13,801 --> 00:06:16,620
Their analysis of what happened had far-reaching effects

124
00:06:16,621 --> 00:06:20,040
in the maritime industry.

125
00:06:20,041 --> 00:06:23,020
Today, there are significant safety features on tankers

126
00:06:23,021 --> 00:06:25,540
to prevent what happened to the Sansonina.

127
00:06:25,541 --> 00:06:28,800
Foremost are inert gas systems.

128
00:06:28,801 --> 00:06:33,720
The theory is that by having no oxygen inside the cargo tank,

129
00:06:33,721 --> 00:06:35,920
it will not support combustion.

130
00:06:35,921 --> 00:06:38,080
If there is insufficient oxygen, there

131
00:06:38,081 --> 00:06:41,480
is no possibility of an explosion or fire.

132
00:06:44,080 --> 00:06:46,720
A typical inert gas system involves taking gases

133
00:06:46,721 --> 00:06:49,300
from the ship's boilers and feeding them into the cargo

134
00:06:49,301 --> 00:06:51,180
tanks.

135
00:06:51,181 --> 00:06:53,380
This drives out all the oxygen.

136
00:06:53,381 --> 00:06:56,560
Without oxygen, vapors left in the tank are inert,

137
00:06:56,561 --> 00:07:00,780
and therefore not combustible.

138
00:07:00,781 --> 00:07:04,380
Even though inert gas systems were in use in the 1970s,

139
00:07:04,381 --> 00:07:08,320
there were no requirements that the Sansonina have one.

140
00:07:08,321 --> 00:07:12,740
Today, no supertanker operates without one.

141
00:07:12,741 --> 00:07:16,040
In addition, venting systems are more sophisticated,

142
00:07:16,041 --> 00:07:19,440
so that hydrocarbon vapors are expelled high above the ship

143
00:07:19,441 --> 00:07:23,320
and unlikely to pool on deck.

144
00:07:23,321 --> 00:07:26,660
Ballasting is still routine, but modern tankers no longer

145
00:07:26,661 --> 00:07:28,640
mix oil and water.

146
00:07:28,641 --> 00:07:31,820
Ballast tanks are separate from cargo tanks.

147
00:07:31,821 --> 00:07:34,460
If you look at the major disasters of history,

148
00:07:34,461 --> 00:07:38,460
you see in retrospect a sequence of mistakes, each one

149
00:07:38,461 --> 00:07:42,800
of which at the time appeared to be innocent and inconsequential,

150
00:07:42,801 --> 00:07:46,260
which all combined in a very unfortunate way

151
00:07:46,261 --> 00:07:50,400
at the time of the disaster to create a disaster.

152
00:07:50,401 --> 00:07:53,720
It took an 18-year-old ship called the Sansonina

153
00:07:53,721 --> 00:07:57,260
and a horrific accident to expose dangers that stalked

154
00:07:57,261 --> 00:08:01,820
oil tankers at sea and at rest in quiet harbors.

155
00:08:01,821 --> 00:08:04,820
July 19, 1979.

156
00:08:04,821 --> 00:08:08,820
The Atlantic Empress spilled 287,000 metric tons of oil

157
00:08:08,821 --> 00:08:11,820
in the Caribbean, the largest oil spill on record.

158
00:08:14,820 --> 00:08:16,820
March 2, 1973.

159
00:08:16,821 --> 00:08:19,820
A Fairfax County, Virginia apartment complex

160
00:08:19,821 --> 00:08:22,820
was nearing completion.

161
00:08:22,821 --> 00:08:24,820
But in the middle of construction,

162
00:08:24,821 --> 00:08:27,820
disaster struck when one of four structures making up the tower

163
00:08:27,821 --> 00:08:30,820
collapsed, killing 14 people.

164
00:08:30,821 --> 00:08:33,620
13 people.

165
00:08:33,621 --> 00:08:37,220
My initial reaction was, what in the world

166
00:08:37,221 --> 00:08:40,420
would make a building that's already gotten up to the 24th

167
00:08:40,421 --> 00:08:44,620
floor all of a sudden come tumbling all the way to the

168
00:08:44,621 --> 00:08:45,920
ground?

169
00:08:45,921 --> 00:08:53,020
I had, I confess, I had absolutely no idea, no theory at all.

170
00:08:53,021 --> 00:08:56,020
The job of determining the specific cause of the collapse

171
00:08:56,021 --> 00:08:59,020
fell to E.V. Leindecker, structural engineer with the

172
00:08:59,021 --> 00:09:02,320
of the National Bureau of Standards and Technology.

173
00:09:02,321 --> 00:09:04,820
Leindecker went to work to determine if the tragedy could

174
00:09:04,821 --> 00:09:07,020
have been a result of poor design.

175
00:09:07,021 --> 00:09:09,820
He saw no immediate shortcomings.

176
00:09:09,821 --> 00:09:12,620
The most important evidence came from workers at the site

177
00:09:12,621 --> 00:09:15,120
who survived the collapse.

178
00:09:15,121 --> 00:09:18,020
From their eyewitness reports, he was able to piece together

179
00:09:18,021 --> 00:09:20,820
a scenario.

180
00:09:20,821 --> 00:09:24,320
His conclusion, the collapse began shortly after a portion

181
00:09:24,321 --> 00:09:28,020
of the 24-story concrete floor slab had been poured.

182
00:09:28,021 --> 00:09:31,620
At the same time, wooden forms used to cast vertical concrete

183
00:09:31,621 --> 00:09:35,520
pillars were being removed two floors below.

184
00:09:35,521 --> 00:09:38,380
According to engineering design recommendations,

185
00:09:38,381 --> 00:09:42,080
those forms were removed too early.

186
00:09:42,081 --> 00:09:45,620
The engineers' drawings had a statement on them

187
00:09:45,621 --> 00:09:49,420
that indicated that under a floor being cast

188
00:09:49,421 --> 00:09:51,620
or where the concrete was being poured,

189
00:09:51,621 --> 00:09:54,080
that you had to have a complete farming system.

190
00:09:54,081 --> 00:09:57,260
That means the wood that's supporting the concrete

191
00:09:57,261 --> 00:10:00,800
had to be in place underneath the slab being cast,

192
00:10:00,801 --> 00:10:04,800
and also had to be in place for one story below that.

193
00:10:04,801 --> 00:10:07,740
The wet concrete being poured on the 24th story

194
00:10:07,741 --> 00:10:10,580
did not have enough support because wooden forms were

195
00:10:10,581 --> 00:10:13,980
removed on floor 22, where vertical concrete pillars

196
00:10:13,981 --> 00:10:15,960
were not completely cured.

197
00:10:15,961 --> 00:10:18,100
Within days of the collapse, the question

198
00:10:18,101 --> 00:10:20,180
became not what happened, but who

199
00:10:20,181 --> 00:10:23,060
was responsible for carrying out the instructions stated

200
00:10:23,061 --> 00:10:26,620
on the engineer's drawings.

201
00:10:26,621 --> 00:10:29,060
Fairfax County building inspectors were not required

202
00:10:29,061 --> 00:10:30,800
to inspect the curing of concrete

203
00:10:30,801 --> 00:10:34,720
before additional floors could be constructed.

204
00:10:34,721 --> 00:10:37,000
It was the job of the contractor to make certain

205
00:10:37,001 --> 00:10:39,000
that vertical columns were secure

206
00:10:39,001 --> 00:10:42,860
before concrete was poured for the new floors.

207
00:10:42,861 --> 00:10:46,140
I could visualize people going in and pulling farms,

208
00:10:46,141 --> 00:10:47,500
and they say, hey, we're great, we're great,

209
00:10:47,501 --> 00:10:50,380
making good progress, let's just keep on going.

210
00:10:50,381 --> 00:10:53,040
That's always been one of my own conjectures,

211
00:10:53,041 --> 00:10:54,580
but that's all that is.

212
00:10:54,581 --> 00:11:01,480
But I think it probably was the inappropriate level of communication between the people running the construction site

213
00:11:01,481 --> 00:11:08,380
and the actual workers that were executing the work, but maybe not having explicit instructions on what they should be doing.

214
00:11:09,200 --> 00:11:14,020
When it became obvious that the skyline collapse had been the result of poor engineering supervision,

215
00:11:14,021 --> 00:11:19,720
the Fairfax District Attorney's Office mobilized to determine if any individuals were criminally liable.

216
00:11:19,721 --> 00:11:23,320
We ultimately presented a case to the grand jury

217
00:11:23,321 --> 00:11:27,180
to charge the superintendent of construction on that day.

218
00:11:27,900 --> 00:11:30,140
We charged him with manslaughter

219
00:11:30,141 --> 00:11:36,680
for what we perceived to be a willful, wanton disregard of human life,

220
00:11:36,760 --> 00:11:38,700
and a jury was never able to resolve it.

221
00:11:39,180 --> 00:11:41,020
The result of the trial was a hung jury,

222
00:11:41,840 --> 00:11:43,800
so there was no conclusion criminally.

223
00:11:44,460 --> 00:11:48,260
The Skyline apartment complex was completely torn down and rebuilt.

224
00:11:48,261 --> 00:11:51,860
The collapse made an impact on building inspectors locally

225
00:11:51,861 --> 00:11:53,240
and across the country.

226
00:11:53,840 --> 00:11:56,440
In the months after this happened,

227
00:11:57,340 --> 00:12:01,300
it seemed to me every high-rise building in the county

228
00:12:01,301 --> 00:12:05,600
under construction had three floors of shoring in place

229
00:12:05,601 --> 00:12:06,480
at all times.

230
00:12:06,740 --> 00:12:08,420
I think this scared the industry.

231
00:12:08,780 --> 00:12:11,960
It resulted in much more concern about safety

232
00:12:11,961 --> 00:12:13,700
than maybe had existed before.

233
00:12:14,640 --> 00:12:17,700
Today, as a direct result of the skyline incident,

234
00:12:17,701 --> 00:12:20,320
building codes and contractor safety guidelines

235
00:12:20,321 --> 00:12:22,560
are more defined and enforced.

236
00:12:23,240 --> 00:12:25,580
No city wants to experience anything like

237
00:12:25,581 --> 00:12:28,120
the Skyline Plaza engineering disaster.

238
00:12:29,900 --> 00:12:32,620
November 10th, 1975.

239
00:12:33,540 --> 00:12:37,400
The 29 crew members of the 729-foot ore carrier,

240
00:12:37,900 --> 00:12:38,820
Edmund Fitzgerald,

241
00:12:39,140 --> 00:12:41,500
were fighting an intense storm on Lake Superior.

242
00:12:42,420 --> 00:12:44,580
Their destination that day was Whitefish Bay

243
00:12:44,581 --> 00:12:46,240
on Michigan's Upper Peninsula.

244
00:12:46,980 --> 00:12:49,240
We're at Whitefish Point just in front of the light station.

245
00:12:49,640 --> 00:12:51,620
Today the winds are only about 20 miles an hour.

246
00:12:51,880 --> 00:12:54,980
Can you imagine what it would have been like with 96-mile-an-hour winds?

247
00:12:55,260 --> 00:12:57,440
These waves are only about 4 or 5 feet today,

248
00:12:57,540 --> 00:12:59,960
but they were 35 feet back in 1975.

249
00:13:01,340 --> 00:13:04,920
Despite the raging storm, the crew was confident in its safety.

250
00:13:05,620 --> 00:13:09,280
The Fitzgerald was one of the strongest and most capable ships on the lakes.

251
00:13:09,520 --> 00:13:12,000
This was a big, powerful boat,

252
00:13:12,260 --> 00:13:15,320
and people really wanted to be on the Edmund Fitzgerald.

253
00:13:15,321 --> 00:13:17,520
This was the one you aspired to serve on.

254
00:13:21,120 --> 00:13:23,980
In fact, when it was launched in 1958,

255
00:13:24,500 --> 00:13:26,860
it was the premier ore carrier on the Great Lakes,

256
00:13:27,400 --> 00:13:29,400
setting records for the largest loads carried

257
00:13:29,401 --> 00:13:30,980
and the fastest trips.

258
00:13:32,560 --> 00:13:35,340
Captain Ernest McSorley was also one of the most experienced

259
00:13:35,341 --> 00:13:38,920
in the business, with 44 years of sailing on the Great Lakes.

260
00:13:40,980 --> 00:13:44,280
But on this day, McSorley was virtually blinded

261
00:13:44,281 --> 00:13:48,380
by two critical navigational system failures.

262
00:13:48,381 --> 00:13:51,680
His first obstacle was the failure of both radar units,

263
00:13:51,681 --> 00:13:57,120
a problem he reported to the nearby ship, Arthur Anderson.

264
00:13:57,121 --> 00:14:00,420
To overcome the loss of radar, the Fitzgerald tried to use

265
00:14:00,421 --> 00:14:03,340
Whitefish Point's radio direction finder,

266
00:14:03,341 --> 00:14:08,680
but the storm had knocked out its power, rendering it useless.

267
00:14:08,681 --> 00:14:12,540
At about 3.30 in the afternoon, McSorley reported to Cooper

268
00:14:12,541 --> 00:14:15,680
that one of the fence rails, part of the sturdy safety fences

269
00:14:15,681 --> 00:14:19,420
on each side of the Fitzgerald, had snapped.

270
00:14:19,421 --> 00:14:21,980
He also said two vent covers were missing,

271
00:14:21,981 --> 00:14:24,580
and the ship was listing to the side,

272
00:14:24,581 --> 00:14:27,980
evidence that water was entering the Fitzgerald.

273
00:14:27,981 --> 00:14:30,560
These were all signs of severe damage,

274
00:14:30,561 --> 00:14:36,100
but the ship soldiered on, pumping out water as it went.

275
00:14:36,101 --> 00:14:39,740
Then at 7.10 PM, the first mate aboard the Arthur Anderson

276
00:14:39,741 --> 00:14:42,780
asked McSorley about the state of his crew.

277
00:14:42,781 --> 00:14:45,620
The response back from Fitzgerald to the Arthur Andersons,

278
00:14:45,621 --> 00:14:47,120
we are holding our own.

279
00:14:47,121 --> 00:14:49,620
That was the last communication between the two ships.

280
00:14:49,621 --> 00:14:51,820
Minutes later, the Edmund Fitzgerald

281
00:14:51,821 --> 00:14:53,320
disappeared from the radar screen

282
00:14:53,321 --> 00:14:54,820
aboard the Arthur Andersons,

283
00:14:54,821 --> 00:14:58,820
without as much as a distress signal.

284
00:14:58,821 --> 00:15:01,700
After a difficult and limited search during the storm

285
00:15:01,701 --> 00:15:03,860
and a larger search the next day,

286
00:15:03,861 --> 00:15:06,860
it was clear the unthinkable had happened.

287
00:15:09,740 --> 00:15:12,740
that the Fitzgerald went down.

288
00:15:12,741 --> 00:15:16,340
And we're talking now about a matter of life

289
00:15:16,341 --> 00:15:19,340
and death and looking for survivors that might be in life

290
00:15:19,341 --> 00:15:21,040
around the world in the waters.

291
00:15:21,041 --> 00:15:24,640
The massive Edmund Fitzgerald and her crew of 29 men

292
00:15:24,641 --> 00:15:25,840
were gone.

293
00:15:25,841 --> 00:15:30,640
We can only ask the masters to...

294
00:15:30,641 --> 00:15:33,740
Six months later, the cameras of a remotely operated

295
00:15:33,741 --> 00:15:36,740
US Navy submersible spotted the wreckage,

296
00:15:36,741 --> 00:15:40,440
agonizingly close to safety, just 17 miles from Whitefish

297
00:15:40,441 --> 00:15:42,940
Point.

298
00:15:42,941 --> 00:15:45,240
Those first images of the wreck and footage

299
00:15:45,241 --> 00:15:47,320
from many other dives over the years

300
00:15:47,321 --> 00:15:49,180
showed that the ship lay in two pieces

301
00:15:49,181 --> 00:15:53,080
on the bottom of Lake Superior, with its back, or stern,

302
00:15:53,081 --> 00:15:57,000
resting upside down.

303
00:15:57,001 --> 00:15:59,100
But locating the wreck did not solve the mystery

304
00:15:59,101 --> 00:16:04,600
of how the ship sank and why the crew never called for help.

305
00:16:04,601 --> 00:16:08,000
More than three decades later, strong opinions still surround

306
00:16:08,001 --> 00:16:10,940
the theories of the ship's tragic fate.

307
00:16:10,941 --> 00:16:13,140
A Coast Guard investigative board looked

308
00:16:13,141 --> 00:16:15,140
into many possibilities.

309
00:16:15,141 --> 00:16:17,840
Probably the only one that they could confidently rule out

310
00:16:17,841 --> 00:16:20,320
once the wreck was discovered was the possibility

311
00:16:20,321 --> 00:16:24,360
the Fitzgerald had broken in half on the surface.

312
00:16:24,361 --> 00:16:27,320
The ship didn't break in half because the bow

313
00:16:27,321 --> 00:16:28,800
would have floated for a while longer,

314
00:16:28,801 --> 00:16:30,260
and the stern would have floated for a while,

315
00:16:30,261 --> 00:16:31,760
and they both would have disappeared

316
00:16:31,761 --> 00:16:34,200
a little bit further apart.

317
00:16:34,201 --> 00:16:38,200
Instead, the two pieces of the ship lay only a few hundred feet apart.

318
00:16:39,080 --> 00:16:44,540
In terms of official findings, the Coast Guard report issued on July 26, 1977,

319
00:16:45,360 --> 00:16:49,740
concluded that water crashing on the deck and leaking through cracked and poorly sealed hatch

320
00:16:49,741 --> 00:16:53,010
covers was the most likely cause of the disaster.

321
00:16:53,011 --> 00:16:56,080
The seals on freighter hatch covers were often

322
00:16:56,081 --> 00:17:01,140
damaged during loading and unloading, and crews often skimped on the tedious process of tightening

323
00:17:01,141 --> 00:17:03,140
of the hatch cover clamps.

324
00:17:03,141 --> 00:17:07,140
Still, many people disagreed with the Coast Guard's theory.

325
00:17:07,141 --> 00:17:09,140
If water was leaking in through the hatch covers,

326
00:17:09,141 --> 00:17:12,140
then the pumps that were on, these 7,000-gallon-per-minute

327
00:17:12,141 --> 00:17:14,140
pumps, and there was two of them going,

328
00:17:14,141 --> 00:17:16,140
should have been able to keep up with as much water

329
00:17:16,141 --> 00:17:19,140
that these hatch covers would allow through leaking.

330
00:17:19,141 --> 00:17:21,140
The Lake Carriers Association,

331
00:17:21,141 --> 00:17:24,140
which represented 15 shipping companies,

332
00:17:24,141 --> 00:17:27,140
protested to the National Transportation Safety Board,

333
00:17:27,141 --> 00:17:31,780
which issued its own findings on May 4, 1978.

334
00:17:31,781 --> 00:17:34,940
The NTSB also focused on the cargo hatches,

335
00:17:34,941 --> 00:17:37,980
but in a different way.

336
00:17:37,981 --> 00:17:39,880
It felt the tremendous weight of the water

337
00:17:39,881 --> 00:17:42,180
crashing across the Fitzgerald's main deck,

338
00:17:42,181 --> 00:17:43,900
known as the spar deck,

339
00:17:43,901 --> 00:17:47,100
forced one or more of the hatches to implode

340
00:17:47,101 --> 00:17:49,100
and swiftly flood the cargo hold.

341
00:17:49,101 --> 00:17:50,900
You can tell by the size of these things,

342
00:17:50,901 --> 00:17:53,400
this 729-foot freighter was...

343
00:17:53,401 --> 00:17:54,700
These things weighed tons,

344
00:17:54,701 --> 00:17:56,200
and it would take an awful lot of weight

345
00:17:56,201 --> 00:17:59,560
to cause them to implode.

346
00:17:59,561 --> 00:18:01,840
Finally, the Lake Carriers Association

347
00:18:01,841 --> 00:18:05,040
proposed its own theory, that the Fitzgerald struck bottom

348
00:18:05,041 --> 00:18:07,640
in the shallow waters around Caribou Island,

349
00:18:07,641 --> 00:18:10,240
a plausible consequence of the ship's navigation system

350
00:18:10,241 --> 00:18:12,960
failures.

351
00:18:12,961 --> 00:18:15,240
Hitting bottom could have caused a rupture in the hull

352
00:18:15,241 --> 00:18:16,960
below the waterline.

353
00:18:16,961 --> 00:18:18,840
The downed fence rail and missing vents

354
00:18:18,841 --> 00:18:21,360
that McSorley reported near Caribou Island

355
00:18:21,361 --> 00:18:23,800
seemed to support that scenario.

356
00:18:23,801 --> 00:18:25,340
This was serious.

357
00:18:25,960 --> 00:18:26,700
How serious?

358
00:18:27,320 --> 00:18:30,900
Not serious enough that McSorley thought he couldn't continue on.

359
00:18:31,400 --> 00:18:32,700
He had his pumps going.

360
00:18:32,780 --> 00:18:34,460
He thought he had it under control.

361
00:18:34,900 --> 00:18:37,120
But it's serious when a fence trail comes down.

362
00:18:37,121 --> 00:18:42,220
That indicates an incredible change in tension in the hull itself.

363
00:18:43,260 --> 00:18:48,420
The type of tension that could result from the middle of the 729-foot ship hitting bottom.

364
00:18:49,660 --> 00:18:52,860
And if you hog a ship, meaning cause it to bend up in the middle,

365
00:18:52,861 --> 00:18:54,960
you can stretch those cables and cause them to snap

366
00:18:54,961 --> 00:18:57,160
because they're supported with stanchions all the way along.

367
00:18:57,161 --> 00:18:59,260
Something's got to give.

368
00:18:59,261 --> 00:19:00,760
But since there are no scrape marks

369
00:19:00,761 --> 00:19:03,500
on the portion of the hull wreckage that can be seen,

370
00:19:03,501 --> 00:19:06,000
bottoming out, like the other explanations,

371
00:19:06,001 --> 00:19:09,420
remains just a theory.

372
00:19:09,421 --> 00:19:11,980
However, most theories at least agree

373
00:19:11,981 --> 00:19:14,280
that the Fitzgerald was taking on water faster

374
00:19:14,281 --> 00:19:16,520
than it could be pumped out.

375
00:19:16,521 --> 00:19:19,560
And the more water that came in, the lower the ship would ride

376
00:19:19,561 --> 00:19:21,320
in those high seas.

377
00:19:21,321 --> 00:19:23,480
The waves were actually boarding the Fitzgerald

378
00:19:23,481 --> 00:19:24,760
on the starboard quarter,

379
00:19:24,761 --> 00:19:27,720
rolling the full length of the deck, the spar deck,

380
00:19:27,721 --> 00:19:29,000
and hitting the back of the pilothouse

381
00:19:29,001 --> 00:19:32,600
and actually forcing this 40-foot-high structure underwater,

382
00:19:32,601 --> 00:19:34,680
and it'd come back up and shake the water off the deck

383
00:19:34,681 --> 00:19:35,800
and keep going.

384
00:19:35,801 --> 00:19:39,000
But one of those dives that did under one of these huge waves

385
00:19:39,001 --> 00:19:39,960
was the final dive,

386
00:19:39,961 --> 00:19:44,520
and it just kept going 535 feet to the bottom.

387
00:19:44,521 --> 00:19:46,840
If McSorley believed the ship would resurface

388
00:19:46,841 --> 00:19:48,520
after that final wave,

389
00:19:48,521 --> 00:19:51,480
Then the first sign of trouble, and the last,

390
00:19:51,481 --> 00:19:54,060
would have been a wall of water bursting through the windows

391
00:19:54,061 --> 00:19:56,060
of the pilothouse from the increased pressure

392
00:19:56,061 --> 00:19:57,860
of deeper waters.

393
00:19:57,861 --> 00:20:01,860
This would explain why there had been no distress call.

394
00:20:01,861 --> 00:20:03,560
But there's still no single theory

395
00:20:03,561 --> 00:20:05,880
about the tragic fate of the Edmund Fitzgerald

396
00:20:05,881 --> 00:20:09,180
that will satisfy everyone.

397
00:20:09,181 --> 00:20:12,220
The bodies of all 29 crewmen remain entombed

398
00:20:12,221 --> 00:20:14,380
in that watery grave.

399
00:20:14,381 --> 00:20:16,380
And at the request of their families,

400
00:20:16,381 --> 00:20:20,480
Divers are no longer permitted near the wreck.

401
00:20:20,481 --> 00:20:23,320
But those relatives do have two special places where they can

402
00:20:23,321 --> 00:20:25,420
remember loved ones.

403
00:20:25,421 --> 00:20:28,520
Just outside the Dawson Great Lakes Museum in Detroit,

404
00:20:28,521 --> 00:20:31,140
an anchor that belonged to the Edmund Fitzgerald prior to its

405
00:20:31,141 --> 00:20:34,220
last voyage is displayed.

406
00:20:34,221 --> 00:20:37,520
And at the Great Lakes Shipwreck Museum at Whitefish Point,

407
00:20:37,521 --> 00:20:39,620
the original bell from the Edmund Fitzgerald

408
00:20:39,621 --> 00:20:42,320
is the centerpiece of the collection.

409
00:20:42,321 --> 00:20:44,140
Known as the voice of the ship, it

410
00:20:44,141 --> 00:20:48,560
It was recovered from the wreckage at the request of the families on July 4, 1995.

411
00:20:49,960 --> 00:20:54,080
When this bell broke the surface after 20 years of darkness and silence,

412
00:20:54,380 --> 00:20:58,380
it actually swung and told, and everybody around for about a mile could hear.

413
00:20:58,480 --> 00:21:01,440
It was one of the most eerie experiences I've ever had,

414
00:21:01,441 --> 00:21:04,600
and the tone itself has such a resonating quality to it

415
00:21:04,601 --> 00:21:06,480
that it's something that you'll never forget.

416
00:21:14,140 --> 00:21:20,060
After the original bell was recovered from the wreckage of the Edmund Fitzgerald,

417
00:21:20,380 --> 00:21:22,312
a replica bell was put in its place.

418
00:21:22,313 --> 00:21:25,640
The names of all 29 crew members are inscribed on the replica,

419
00:21:26,160 --> 00:21:28,220
which serves as a permanent grave marker.

420
00:21:31,260 --> 00:21:32,649
February 26, 1972.

421
00:21:32,650 --> 00:21:38,360
A dangerous flood ripped through the rugged Appalachian Valley communities

422
00:21:38,361 --> 00:21:40,710
along Buffalo Creek, West Virginia.

423
00:21:40,711 --> 00:21:44,000
Civilians in the surrounding area of Logan County

424
00:21:44,001 --> 00:21:47,760
tried to escape, but the powerful torrent turned fatal.

425
00:21:48,820 --> 00:21:52,460
125 people died, and over 1,000 were injured

426
00:21:52,461 --> 00:21:53,800
in the devastating tragedy.

427
00:21:56,940 --> 00:21:59,540
Logan County was one of the most prolific producers

428
00:21:59,541 --> 00:22:03,000
of coal in the United States, but its history is now linked

429
00:22:03,001 --> 00:22:06,300
to a deadly engineering disaster that forever altered life

430
00:22:06,301 --> 00:22:07,440
in the coal mining community.

431
00:22:09,680 --> 00:22:12,140
Coal mining has always been a dangerous activity.

432
00:22:12,141 --> 00:22:17,140
methane poisoning, cave-ins, and explosions

433
00:22:17,240 --> 00:22:20,140
are just some of the hazards that plague miners.

434
00:22:20,240 --> 00:22:24,180
Over the decades, hard-fought gains in safety conditions

435
00:22:24,280 --> 00:22:26,780
and increased automation made coal mining

436
00:22:26,880 --> 00:22:28,900
a somewhat less hazardous job.

437
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:31,500
But one aspect of the coal mining operation

438
00:22:31,600 --> 00:22:37,000
remained dangerously unchecked, coal waste dams.

439
00:22:37,100 --> 00:22:39,900
Unlike traditional dams, coal waste dams

440
00:22:39,901 --> 00:22:44,464
Dam's provide neither electricity nor a reservoir of potable water.

441
00:22:44,465 --> 00:22:46,440
They are essentially gigantic

442
00:22:46,441 --> 00:22:47,468
garbage dumps.

443
00:22:47,469 --> 00:22:53,560
You mine coal, and coal usually has some material in it that's not coal. You run it

444
00:22:53,561 --> 00:22:58,653
through different processes, and you end up with coal, and then you end up with refuse.

445
00:22:58,654 --> 00:22:59,354
And these

446
00:22:59,355 --> 00:23:01,273
processes include the use of water.

447
00:23:01,274 --> 00:23:04,980
So now you have, you have, you have refuse you have to get rid

448
00:23:04,981 --> 00:23:07,220
of the water you have to get rid of.

449
00:23:07,221 --> 00:23:09,720
For years, mining companies dumped the wastewater

450
00:23:09,721 --> 00:23:13,180
directly into streams, including Buffalo Creek.

451
00:23:13,181 --> 00:23:16,720
But early environmental laws stopped that.

452
00:23:16,721 --> 00:23:19,560
Companies were required to at least make an effort

453
00:23:19,561 --> 00:23:22,900
to try to control the wastewater from the plant.

454
00:23:22,901 --> 00:23:24,160
The cheapest thing for them to do

455
00:23:24,161 --> 00:23:28,200
was just to build a dam across the valley.

456
00:23:28,201 --> 00:23:30,880
In 1960, the Laredo Mining Company

457
00:23:30,881 --> 00:23:33,740
built what became known as Dam Number One,

458
00:23:33,741 --> 00:23:36,780
overlooking the narrow valley of Buffalo Creek.

459
00:23:36,880 --> 00:23:40,140
The dam was built of coal waste, also called gob,

460
00:23:40,240 --> 00:23:42,140
which smoldered and often caught fire

461
00:23:42,240 --> 00:23:45,480
due to its high carbon content.

462
00:23:45,580 --> 00:23:48,380
When Number One's reservoir became filled with silt,

463
00:23:48,480 --> 00:23:51,660
the mining company built a larger dam, Number Two,

464
00:23:51,760 --> 00:23:56,100
right behind it on soft clay soil with little shear strength.

465
00:23:56,200 --> 00:23:59,240
Several problems plagued the first two dams.

466
00:23:59,340 --> 00:24:01,600
Dam Number One overtopped several times,

467
00:24:01,601 --> 00:24:03,400
and there were black water discharges

468
00:24:03,401 --> 00:24:05,540
that came into Buffalo Creek.

469
00:24:06,120 --> 00:24:07,560
Dam number two failed.

470
00:24:07,760 --> 00:24:09,980
The front face of it failed and split in the middle

471
00:24:09,981 --> 00:24:14,220
a couple of times in the late 60s.

472
00:24:15,400 --> 00:24:17,740
The U.S. Geological Survey reported

473
00:24:17,741 --> 00:24:19,960
that the dams were vulnerable to a washout.

474
00:24:20,600 --> 00:24:23,560
There was evidence of foundation failure piping

475
00:24:23,561 --> 00:24:27,600
in both structures, particularly in dam number two,

476
00:24:27,860 --> 00:24:30,960
but the company went ahead and built the third dam anyway.

477
00:24:31,600 --> 00:24:36,180
Dam number three was to be massive, 500 feet thick

478
00:24:36,181 --> 00:24:39,580
in the middle, stretching 465 feet across the valley

479
00:24:39,581 --> 00:24:43,160
and rising 60 feet above dam number two's impoundment bond.

480
00:24:44,520 --> 00:24:47,460
Despite the dam size and the fact that it was to be built

481
00:24:47,461 --> 00:24:50,460
overlooking a floodplain home to several small towns,

482
00:24:50,900 --> 00:24:54,940
the only engineering plan was this rudimentary sketch.

483
00:24:55,540 --> 00:24:58,500
At the time, engineering requirements for coal dams

484
00:24:58,501 --> 00:25:01,080
and federal enforcement were sorely lacking.

485
00:25:01,081 --> 00:25:05,380
There was just one provision that said any refuse embankments

486
00:25:05,381 --> 00:25:08,080
and impoundments should be substantially constructed.

487
00:25:08,220 --> 00:25:09,900
That was it, one paragraph.

488
00:25:10,920 --> 00:25:15,480
Bulldozers began forming the giant face of Dam No. 3 in 1968.

489
00:25:16,440 --> 00:25:18,800
The coarse refuse material was not compacted.

490
00:25:18,920 --> 00:25:23,820
It was dumped by large trucks and then pushed into the center of the valley

491
00:25:23,821 --> 00:25:28,000
out on top of the slurry that had been discharged by the previous dams.

492
00:25:28,001 --> 00:25:31,340
There's no way one could get compaction with that thick layer

493
00:25:31,440 --> 00:25:33,740
of material being pushed forward.

494
00:25:33,840 --> 00:25:37,200
So there was no strength whatsoever in the material.

495
00:25:37,300 --> 00:25:40,240
In addition, the dam was built without proper spillways

496
00:25:40,340 --> 00:25:43,240
to alleviate the reservoir when it reached capacity.

497
00:25:43,340 --> 00:25:45,660
Such basic engineering deficiencies

498
00:25:45,760 --> 00:25:48,260
made a catastrophe virtually inevitable.

499
00:25:48,360 --> 00:25:52,800
The front face of the dam failed, I believe, in 1969

500
00:25:52,900 --> 00:25:54,600
and again in 1971.

501
00:25:54,700 --> 00:25:56,960
So there have been two failures of the front face

502
00:25:56,961 --> 00:26:01,600
of Dam No. 3 prior to February 26, 1972.

503
00:26:01,700 --> 00:26:03,460
Anybody with engineering knowledge

504
00:26:03,560 --> 00:26:05,800
knew that there were some serious problems

505
00:26:05,900 --> 00:26:09,200
with Dam No. 3 and its long-term stability.

506
00:26:09,300 --> 00:26:11,800
Despite such evidence, the Pittston Company,

507
00:26:11,900 --> 00:26:14,080
the dam's final owners, did not make

508
00:26:14,180 --> 00:26:17,520
the needed structural changes.

509
00:26:17,620 --> 00:26:22,060
8 AM, February 26, 1972.

510
00:26:22,160 --> 00:26:25,160
Four years of runoff from the mine and seasonal rains

511
00:26:25,161 --> 00:26:28,760
had swollen the dam's reservoir to 132 million gallons.

512
00:26:30,140 --> 00:26:32,960
Anxious residents noted large cracks in the dam's face.

513
00:26:33,860 --> 00:26:35,280
The crest had softened.

514
00:26:35,680 --> 00:26:37,200
The mining company officials,

515
00:26:37,560 --> 00:26:39,820
even though they had no real engineering knowledge,

516
00:26:39,821 --> 00:26:41,960
took it upon themselves to tell people

517
00:26:41,961 --> 00:26:43,440
that there was no danger

518
00:26:43,441 --> 00:26:45,740
and they should go home or stay in their homes.

519
00:26:46,240 --> 00:26:48,200
And it was only some local residents

520
00:26:48,201 --> 00:26:50,580
who actually warned their neighbors

521
00:26:50,581 --> 00:26:52,100
and got some people out.

522
00:26:52,220 --> 00:26:53,780
Somebody did call my mother

523
00:26:53,781 --> 00:26:57,020
and told her that there was fear, I guess,

524
00:26:57,120 --> 00:26:59,020
up on the creek that the dam would break.

525
00:26:59,120 --> 00:27:00,780
So we kind of knew something was going on.

526
00:27:00,880 --> 00:27:04,320
We didn't have any clue how bad it was going to be, though.

527
00:27:04,420 --> 00:27:07,520
Water had saturated so deeply into the dam's interior

528
00:27:07,620 --> 00:27:10,800
that a third of its face slid off into the impoundment pond

529
00:27:10,900 --> 00:27:13,540
of dam number two.

530
00:27:13,640 --> 00:27:16,700
The remainder of the dam then failed catastrophically.

531
00:27:16,800 --> 00:27:20,540
It simply liquefied, unleashing massive amounts of energy

532
00:27:20,541 --> 00:27:24,040
as it overtopped dams one and two on its unstoppable path

533
00:27:24,041 --> 00:27:26,340
to the valley below.

534
00:27:26,341 --> 00:27:28,840
The flood announced itself with loud explosions

535
00:27:28,841 --> 00:27:30,960
as it slammed into the smoldering gob pile

536
00:27:30,961 --> 00:27:32,960
that was dam number one.

537
00:27:32,961 --> 00:27:35,880
The town of Saunders was directly below.

538
00:27:35,881 --> 00:27:39,660
Within a matter of moments, the whole town of Saunders

539
00:27:39,661 --> 00:27:44,300
was obliterated by this mass of water and slurry.

540
00:27:44,301 --> 00:27:46,500
The 20-foot-high floodway surged

541
00:27:46,501 --> 00:27:49,540
with 132 million gallons of water

542
00:27:49,541 --> 00:27:52,380
and 1 million gallons of sludge.

543
00:27:52,381 --> 00:27:54,220
It gained momentum from gravity

544
00:27:54,221 --> 00:27:56,220
and from its own increasing mass

545
00:27:56,221 --> 00:27:58,720
as it grabbed up mud, coal waste,

546
00:27:58,721 --> 00:28:01,720
and soon, houses and cars.

547
00:28:01,721 --> 00:28:03,720
I could see the creek, and I could see all this black water

548
00:28:03,721 --> 00:28:06,720
coming down it and thinking, we got some type of flood.

549
00:28:06,721 --> 00:28:08,720
And then I seen that white house float down

550
00:28:08,721 --> 00:28:10,560
and hit the bridge.

551
00:28:10,561 --> 00:28:12,560
And, you know, the house just splintered all the pieces,

552
00:28:12,561 --> 00:28:14,560
and the water backed up and started coming over

553
00:28:14,561 --> 00:28:16,060
toward where I was at.

554
00:28:16,061 --> 00:28:18,260
As some scrambled up the cold, muddy hillsides

555
00:28:18,261 --> 00:28:21,600
to escape, they looked back to see houses floating by,

556
00:28:21,601 --> 00:28:24,500
with people still looking out the windows.

557
00:28:24,501 --> 00:28:28,000
These houses were soon smashed to bits along overpasses

558
00:28:28,001 --> 00:28:30,000
and railroad trestles.

559
00:28:30,001 --> 00:28:31,800
When the trestle started breaking,

560
00:28:31,801 --> 00:28:33,800
I heard a lot of people screaming.

561
00:28:33,801 --> 00:28:37,920
And we found out later that there were people on the trestle

562
00:28:37,921 --> 00:28:39,420
when it broke.

563
00:28:39,421 --> 00:28:41,220
The torrent of water and waste

564
00:28:41,221 --> 00:28:43,420
cut a 17-mile swath of destruction

565
00:28:43,421 --> 00:28:46,420
through the 17 small towns along Buffalo Creek

566
00:28:46,421 --> 00:28:49,320
before joining the Guyondot River.

567
00:28:49,420 --> 00:28:53,420
It was all over by 11 a.m.

568
00:28:53,520 --> 00:28:56,220
125 people died.

569
00:28:56,320 --> 00:28:58,160
1,000 more were injured.

570
00:28:58,260 --> 00:29:04,280
507 houses, 44 mobile homes, and 30 businesses were destroyed.

571
00:29:04,380 --> 00:29:07,680
4,000 people were left homeless.

572
00:29:07,780 --> 00:29:10,980
It was like you had civilization one day,

573
00:29:11,080 --> 00:29:13,680
and then it was pure chaos the next day.

574
00:29:13,681 --> 00:29:17,440
And that chaos lasted for a very, very, very long time.

575
00:29:18,300 --> 00:29:20,600
Entire families died together.

576
00:29:21,340 --> 00:29:27,200
There was friends and friends of friends and relatives of relatives that died.

577
00:29:29,700 --> 00:29:31,500
A lot of funerals to go to.

578
00:29:36,060 --> 00:29:37,800
Rage followed grief.

579
00:29:37,801 --> 00:29:41,620
They take the co-operators' money and elect you politicians,

580
00:29:41,621 --> 00:29:44,460
and you get to Washington and you care nothing about her.

581
00:29:44,900 --> 00:29:46,980
As with other disasters of this scale,

582
00:29:47,360 --> 00:29:50,120
the Buffalo Creek Dam disaster was a shocking wake-up call

583
00:29:50,121 --> 00:29:51,640
to federal and state agencies.

584
00:29:52,600 --> 00:29:56,160
The Army Corps of Engineers and the Mine Safety and Health Administration,

585
00:29:56,540 --> 00:30:00,980
or MSHA, launched a comprehensive inspection of coal waste dams across the nation.

586
00:30:01,720 --> 00:30:06,320
We felt that we needed to have an engineering analysis of what was available

587
00:30:06,321 --> 00:30:10,120
and be able to tell the mining community,

588
00:30:10,121 --> 00:30:13,300
if you're going to build these things, here's what you got to do.

589
00:30:13,860 --> 00:30:17,820
Out of the horror of Buffalo Creek came a remarkable era of dam improvement

590
00:30:17,821 --> 00:30:20,720
where before almost no regulations existed.

591
00:30:22,620 --> 00:30:25,580
MSHA and other agencies wrote detailed engineering specifications.

592
00:30:26,420 --> 00:30:30,560
They demonstrated how proper engineering combined with vigilant public scrutiny

593
00:30:30,561 --> 00:30:34,400
can prevent a tragedy like Buffalo Creek from happening again.

594
00:30:34,401 --> 00:30:42,440
Four days before the disaster, a federal mine inspector and the company safety engineer

595
00:30:42,441 --> 00:30:45,560
declared Buffalo Creek's dams satisfactory.

596
00:30:47,160 --> 00:30:49,820
December 22nd, 1977.

597
00:30:50,720 --> 00:30:55,780
It was just after 9 a.m., a quiet morning in the small southern city of West Wego, Louisiana,

598
00:30:56,140 --> 00:31:00,620
when suddenly a 250-foot grain elevator exploded.

599
00:31:00,621 --> 00:31:05,020
It was heard by people over 10 miles away from the facility.

600
00:31:05,300 --> 00:31:06,880
The energy released in this explosion

601
00:31:06,881 --> 00:31:09,020
was equivalent to many hundreds,

602
00:31:09,280 --> 00:31:11,120
if not thousands of pounds of TNT.

603
00:31:11,700 --> 00:31:15,580
There was fire and smoke coming from the tops of the silos.

604
00:31:16,160 --> 00:31:17,240
There was debris everywhere.

605
00:31:17,520 --> 00:31:20,000
It was total devastation.

606
00:31:21,160 --> 00:31:23,760
Even more shocking than the magnitude of the explosion

607
00:31:23,761 --> 00:31:26,460
was its cause, grain dust.

608
00:31:26,461 --> 00:31:31,800
Any material that can burn will burn rapidly and explosively

609
00:31:31,801 --> 00:31:33,400
if it's small enough.

610
00:31:33,620 --> 00:31:35,820
Grain produces dust when it's handled.

611
00:31:36,280 --> 00:31:39,280
That dust burns so rapidly it's explosive.

612
00:31:40,720 --> 00:31:43,760
And when fine dust particles are suspended in air,

613
00:31:44,240 --> 00:31:47,560
surrounded by oxygen, the mixture becomes highly volatile.

614
00:31:49,300 --> 00:31:51,800
Because of the design, grain elevators

615
00:31:51,801 --> 00:31:53,760
are highly susceptible to dust explosions.

616
00:31:53,761 --> 00:31:56,760
The actual elevator at a facility typically consists

617
00:31:56,761 --> 00:31:58,760
of a multitude of buckets connected

618
00:31:58,761 --> 00:32:00,760
to a vertical conveyor belt.

619
00:32:00,761 --> 00:32:04,760
When a barge is unloaded, the bucket elevator scoops up grain

620
00:32:04,761 --> 00:32:07,760
and carries it to the head house.

621
00:32:07,761 --> 00:32:10,760
From there, it's sorted by grain type

622
00:32:10,761 --> 00:32:13,760
and dropped into concrete silos.

623
00:32:13,761 --> 00:32:16,760
Grain elevators are designed to move tremendous quantities

624
00:32:16,761 --> 00:32:17,760
of grain.

625
00:32:17,761 --> 00:32:19,760
When you handle these materials,

626
00:32:19,761 --> 00:32:21,760
the generation of dust is inevitable.

627
00:32:21,761 --> 00:32:28,180
If you look at the four factors required for a dust explosion, the presence of dust is ever-present.

628
00:32:28,520 --> 00:32:33,280
We have enclosure or confinement of the structure itself and oxygen in the air.

629
00:32:34,000 --> 00:32:38,420
Therefore, all that's needed is a spark or ignition source to have a dust explosion.

630
00:32:43,820 --> 00:32:48,940
On the morning of December 22nd, an unusual cold front was passing through West Wego,

631
00:32:49,260 --> 00:32:51,460
dropping the humidity to a very low level.

632
00:32:51,760 --> 00:32:55,260
Both static electricity and grain dust were in the air,

633
00:32:55,261 --> 00:32:57,760
making the unthinkable possible.

634
00:32:57,761 --> 00:33:01,260
About 9.15, I heard an explosion,

635
00:33:01,261 --> 00:33:05,260
and my daughter and I went up to the river road.

636
00:33:05,261 --> 00:33:07,760
All we could see was a lot of dust

637
00:33:07,761 --> 00:33:10,760
and a lot of concrete all over the place.

638
00:33:10,761 --> 00:33:13,760
There were people walking around covered in grain dust.

639
00:33:13,761 --> 00:33:16,260
Many of them were digging through debris,

640
00:33:16,261 --> 00:33:19,760
trying to find co-workers and things like that.

641
00:33:19,761 --> 00:33:23,100
that helicopters were there probably within 30 to 40

642
00:33:23,101 --> 00:33:24,840
minutes after the blast.

643
00:33:24,841 --> 00:33:28,000
And they were trying to rescue people off the tops of the silos.

644
00:33:28,001 --> 00:33:31,940
The radio said it was the river side of the green elevator.

645
00:33:31,941 --> 00:33:36,280
When we got there, it was the head house that blew.

646
00:33:36,281 --> 00:33:39,000
The fractured concrete that rained down on the head house

647
00:33:39,001 --> 00:33:42,280
control room killed 36.

648
00:33:42,281 --> 00:33:44,140
The shock wave from the first explosion

649
00:33:44,141 --> 00:33:47,020
traveled through the facility, causing additional dust

650
00:33:47,021 --> 00:33:50,720
to become airborne and to combust.

651
00:33:50,721 --> 00:33:54,820
A number of secondary explosions ignited instantaneously,

652
00:33:54,821 --> 00:33:58,460
completely destroying the facility.

653
00:33:58,461 --> 00:34:01,200
The explosion in West Wego involved

654
00:34:01,201 --> 00:34:03,840
a very large quantity of dust built up

655
00:34:03,841 --> 00:34:05,700
in a very large structure.

656
00:34:05,701 --> 00:34:08,200
Due to the magnitude of this explosion,

657
00:34:08,201 --> 00:34:11,080
the exact cause cannot be pinpointed,

658
00:34:11,081 --> 00:34:14,440
but is likely a malfunctioning mechanical component

659
00:34:14,441 --> 00:34:17,240
on the bucket elevator, such as a failed bearing.

660
00:34:17,241 --> 00:34:18,820
The resulting mechanical friction

661
00:34:18,821 --> 00:34:21,680
would have produced a fatal spark or enough heat

662
00:34:21,681 --> 00:34:24,420
to cause the dust to ignite.

663
00:34:24,421 --> 00:34:27,580
The West Wego disaster remains the deadliest grain dust

664
00:34:27,581 --> 00:34:30,480
explosion on record.

665
00:34:30,481 --> 00:34:33,120
In the years to follow, the grain elevator industry

666
00:34:33,121 --> 00:34:36,460
has taken extensive measures to minimize the potential for grain

667
00:34:36,461 --> 00:34:37,420
dust explosions.

668
00:34:40,760 --> 00:34:43,600
The Bungie Grain Elevator in Destrehan, Louisiana,

669
00:34:43,601 --> 00:34:45,720
serves as an example of new safety methods

670
00:34:45,721 --> 00:34:48,480
implemented throughout the industry.

671
00:34:48,481 --> 00:34:53,120
The incidence of 1977 in the grain industry

672
00:34:53,121 --> 00:34:55,300
was kind of a wake-up call, if you will,

673
00:34:55,301 --> 00:34:57,040
for the whole industry to realize

674
00:34:57,041 --> 00:35:01,040
that we had to give a renewed focus and much more emphasis

675
00:35:01,041 --> 00:35:07,460
on root cause analysis, what causes these type of explosions.

676
00:35:07,461 --> 00:35:09,040
One of the critical safety features

677
00:35:09,041 --> 00:35:11,760
implemented by the industry is to closely monitor

678
00:35:11,761 --> 00:35:15,860
potential ignition points, primarily the bucket elevator,

679
00:35:15,861 --> 00:35:20,260
the cause of 40% of all green dust explosions.

680
00:35:20,261 --> 00:35:24,060
If a bearing overheats or the conveyor belt is misaligned,

681
00:35:24,061 --> 00:35:26,820
heat sensors located throughout the elevator legs

682
00:35:26,821 --> 00:35:29,860
will notify personnel of a potential explosion.

683
00:35:29,861 --> 00:35:34,060
We have over 700 of these discrete points

684
00:35:34,061 --> 00:35:36,500
that we're actually monitoring the temperature all the time.

685
00:35:36,501 --> 00:35:39,900
And that information is conveyed back to the control room

686
00:35:39,901 --> 00:35:41,620
that is sensing these temperatures,

687
00:35:41,621 --> 00:35:43,900
giving us alarm if it gets to a certain level

688
00:35:43,901 --> 00:35:46,440
and can even shut down the equipment automatically

689
00:35:46,441 --> 00:35:48,740
if it gets to a higher level.

690
00:35:48,741 --> 00:35:51,640
However, if there is an accidental explosion,

691
00:35:51,641 --> 00:35:54,660
measures have been taken to contain the blast wave,

692
00:35:54,661 --> 00:35:58,020
preventing the often deadlier secondary explosions.

693
00:35:58,021 --> 00:36:01,820
Three, two, one.

694
00:36:01,821 --> 00:36:04,720
How it works is that the suppression device

695
00:36:04,721 --> 00:36:07,580
actually senses the increase in pressure

696
00:36:07,581 --> 00:36:10,620
by the early stages of an explosion

697
00:36:10,621 --> 00:36:15,060
and would dump a chemical into that bucket elevator casing

698
00:36:15,061 --> 00:36:19,820
that would immediately suppress and eliminate the explosion.

699
00:36:19,821 --> 00:36:23,460
The chemical agent is dispersed within 80 milliseconds,

700
00:36:23,461 --> 00:36:27,100
stopping the explosion in its tracks.

701
00:36:27,101 --> 00:36:29,740
And as a direct result of the Westwego incident,

702
00:36:29,741 --> 00:36:32,500
grain elevator head houses are no longer located

703
00:36:32,501 --> 00:36:34,580
above employee offices.

704
00:36:34,581 --> 00:36:35,780
Spreading the facilities out,

705
00:36:35,781 --> 00:36:39,780
out moving the control rooms to remote locations. All of those came at a cost,

706
00:36:39,781 --> 00:36:43,780
efficiency, but was worth it from the safety payback.

707
00:36:43,781 --> 00:36:47,780
We went from a time in a peak year over 60 people

708
00:36:47,781 --> 00:36:51,780
were killed in agricultural dust explosions in the late 70s,

709
00:36:51,781 --> 00:36:55,780
and a great reduction in risk has been achieved through the engineering evaluation

710
00:36:55,781 --> 00:36:59,780
of these types of accidents and taking appropriate steps based on the knowledge learned.

711
00:36:59,781 --> 00:37:02,280
knowledge learned.

712
00:37:02,281 --> 00:37:06,380
December 27, 1977, five days after the green elevator

713
00:37:06,381 --> 00:37:08,700
explosion in West Wego, Louisiana,

714
00:37:08,701 --> 00:37:10,580
a green elevator in Galveston, Texas,

715
00:37:10,581 --> 00:37:15,660
also exploded, taking the lives of 18 people.

716
00:37:15,661 --> 00:37:20,380
August 13, 1979, the Rosemont Horizon Arena

717
00:37:20,381 --> 00:37:22,300
suffered a major structural failure

718
00:37:22,301 --> 00:37:26,720
and collapsed in the middle of its construction.

719
00:37:26,721 --> 00:37:29,260
They are the modern-day versions of the Roman Colosseum,

720
00:37:30,380 --> 00:37:32,120
built to house and entertain thousands.

721
00:37:32,840 --> 00:37:35,820
But engineering mistakes can lead to a disaster.

722
00:37:37,040 --> 00:37:41,160
In the late 1970s, arena collapses became eerily common.

723
00:37:42,080 --> 00:37:44,720
In 1978, the Hartford Civic Center

724
00:37:44,721 --> 00:37:45,960
suffered a structural failure

725
00:37:45,961 --> 00:37:48,000
just hours after hosting a sporting event.

726
00:37:49,000 --> 00:37:53,580
In 1979, the Kemper Sports Arena also came crashing down.

727
00:37:54,380 --> 00:37:56,240
Both arenas had a common point of failure.

728
00:37:56,720 --> 00:37:57,720
the roof.

729
00:37:57,721 --> 00:38:02,920
What normally causes roof failures really has two origins.

730
00:38:02,921 --> 00:38:07,740
When structures that have been completed fail, they fail either because they're overloaded

731
00:38:07,741 --> 00:38:15,160
or because there's some problem of deterioration perhaps or some other issue where the structure's

732
00:38:15,161 --> 00:38:19,720
performance under load is different than the assumptions.

733
00:38:19,721 --> 00:38:25,760
The roofs that never make it out of construction, those roofs collapse during construction and

734
00:38:25,761 --> 00:38:27,620
One of the most prevalent causes

735
00:38:27,621 --> 00:38:31,140
are some form of instability.

736
00:38:31,141 --> 00:38:35,940
Such was the case in 1979 with the Rosemont Horizon.

737
00:38:35,941 --> 00:38:39,740
In 1978, the village of Rosemont, Illinois, a Chicago

738
00:38:39,741 --> 00:38:43,940
suburb, broke ground on an ambitious 18,000 seat capacity

739
00:38:43,941 --> 00:38:46,360
sports complex.

740
00:38:46,361 --> 00:38:48,920
The decision to build the arena was largely influenced

741
00:38:48,921 --> 00:38:52,780
by Rosemont's close proximity to Chicago's O'Hare Airport,

742
00:38:52,781 --> 00:38:55,240
one of the busiest in the world.

743
00:38:55,241 --> 00:38:57,780
But the airport was also responsible for a great deal

744
00:38:57,781 --> 00:39:01,240
of noise pollution, enough to demand a unique type of roof

745
00:39:01,241 --> 00:39:02,880
that utilized wood.

746
00:39:02,881 --> 00:39:06,420
It is practically dead center of one of the glide paths

747
00:39:06,421 --> 00:39:08,080
coming in on the runways.

748
00:39:08,081 --> 00:39:11,760
So the wood was used for sound insulation.

749
00:39:11,761 --> 00:39:14,100
A design for the roof of the Rosemont Horizon

750
00:39:14,101 --> 00:39:17,020
called for 16 glue laminated arches,

751
00:39:17,021 --> 00:39:19,520
each spanning 288 feet.

752
00:39:19,521 --> 00:39:22,660
If you can keep the arch in a single plane,

753
00:39:22,661 --> 00:39:24,200
like a piece of paper.

754
00:39:24,201 --> 00:39:26,760
It is a very efficient structural system,

755
00:39:26,761 --> 00:39:29,100
one that's very strong for its weight.

756
00:39:29,101 --> 00:39:31,300
The thing that's important to recognize, though,

757
00:39:31,301 --> 00:39:34,640
is you must make efforts to keep it in plane.

758
00:39:34,641 --> 00:39:37,640
As soon as the arch begins to move out of plane,

759
00:39:37,641 --> 00:39:40,340
in other words, to twist or to rotate,

760
00:39:40,341 --> 00:39:42,420
it immediately begins to lose strength.

761
00:39:42,421 --> 00:39:46,860
It creates conditions which allows the arch to behave badly,

762
00:39:46,861 --> 00:39:49,420
and ultimately, it will collapse.

763
00:39:49,421 --> 00:39:50,980
Each arch of the roof was fashioned

764
00:39:50,981 --> 00:39:53,080
from three distinct pieces.

765
00:39:53,081 --> 00:39:57,220
Two entrances were connected to two concrete buttresses,

766
00:39:57,221 --> 00:40:00,320
and then the crown trust was placed in the middle.

767
00:40:00,321 --> 00:40:03,380
The two end pieces were then spliced to the centerpiece

768
00:40:03,381 --> 00:40:06,060
by prefabricated metal sheeting.

769
00:40:06,061 --> 00:40:08,320
Running perpendicular between the arches

770
00:40:08,321 --> 00:40:12,080
was a series of wood beams known as purlins.

771
00:40:12,081 --> 00:40:14,620
Those purlins serve two functions.

772
00:40:14,621 --> 00:40:17,720
They support the roof decking and the roof materials,

773
00:40:17,721 --> 00:40:20,100
but more importantly, they laterally

774
00:40:20,101 --> 00:40:23,600
support the arch, which is a very slender element.

775
00:40:23,601 --> 00:40:27,480
The arches are absolutely critically supported

776
00:40:27,481 --> 00:40:29,480
by the purlins.

777
00:40:29,481 --> 00:40:31,440
As the arches were being assembled,

778
00:40:31,441 --> 00:40:33,420
problems quickly arose.

779
00:40:33,421 --> 00:40:36,780
The arch sections didn't line up with the splice holes.

780
00:40:36,781 --> 00:40:38,920
Also, the spacing between the arches

781
00:40:38,921 --> 00:40:42,120
was off by as much as 12 inches in one span,

782
00:40:42,121 --> 00:40:44,560
making the purlins difficult to place.

783
00:40:44,561 --> 00:40:47,400
And because of the misalignment, some of the connection holes

784
00:40:47,401 --> 00:40:50,560
were enlarged, and many of the bolts were omitted.

785
00:40:50,561 --> 00:40:54,140
Temporary bolts called drift pins were used instead.

786
00:40:54,141 --> 00:40:56,300
One of the principal differences between a wood

787
00:40:56,301 --> 00:41:00,160
structure and a steel structure relates to the connections

788
00:41:00,161 --> 00:41:02,060
in the bolting processes.

789
00:41:02,061 --> 00:41:07,960
The erectors in the Chicago area would rely on steel erectors,

790
00:41:07,961 --> 00:41:10,140
people that are familiar with such structures

791
00:41:10,141 --> 00:41:14,100
as the Sears Tower, John Hancock, and other notable

792
00:41:14,101 --> 00:41:15,340
steel structures.

793
00:41:15,341 --> 00:41:19,180
In those structures, it's common to not employ all the bolts,

794
00:41:19,181 --> 00:41:21,400
to put all the bolts in the connection,

795
00:41:21,401 --> 00:41:23,740
because of the difficulty in fitting up

796
00:41:23,741 --> 00:41:25,620
a multi-bolted connection.

797
00:41:25,621 --> 00:41:29,420
In wood, we have to be careful that all of those bolts may

798
00:41:29,421 --> 00:41:33,580
be necessary for our delicate friend, the arch, who

799
00:41:33,581 --> 00:41:37,660
has a tendency to get out of plane.

800
00:41:37,661 --> 00:41:41,560
In the early morning hours of August 13, 1979,

801
00:41:41,561 --> 00:41:44,040
the final arch of the roof was being erected

802
00:41:44,041 --> 00:41:47,200
when a 20-mile-per-hour gust of wind blowing perpendicular

803
00:41:47,201 --> 00:41:49,880
to the arches led to disaster.

804
00:41:49,881 --> 00:41:52,540
The arches became unstable and collapsed,

805
00:41:52,541 --> 00:41:54,080
moving toward the west.

806
00:41:54,081 --> 00:41:58,380
And it formed somewhat of a progressive-type collapse

807
00:41:58,381 --> 00:42:02,380
in which one arch failed, released the lateral bracing

808
00:42:02,381 --> 00:42:05,100
to the next arch, loaded it laterally.

809
00:42:05,101 --> 00:42:08,340
It failed, then the next, then the next, then the next.

810
00:42:08,341 --> 00:42:10,540
The timber roof fell 60 feet,

811
00:42:10,541 --> 00:42:13,380
splintering like a pile of matchsticks.

812
00:42:13,381 --> 00:42:16,220
construction workers were killed in the accident.

813
00:42:16,221 --> 00:42:19,220
The arena was completely destroyed.

814
00:42:19,221 --> 00:42:22,380
An investigation into the failure of the Rosemont Horizon

815
00:42:22,381 --> 00:42:27,120
found that 54% of the 966 required bolts

816
00:42:27,121 --> 00:42:28,780
were missing from the roof.

817
00:42:28,781 --> 00:42:35,140
Of the 444 bolts that were in place, 338 had no nuts.

818
00:42:35,141 --> 00:42:39,380
The principal reasons for the collapse of the roof

819
00:42:39,381 --> 00:42:42,940
relate to the absence of lateral bracing

820
00:42:42,941 --> 00:42:44,840
on the main arch members.

821
00:42:44,841 --> 00:42:48,220
That lateral bracing is accomplished

822
00:42:48,221 --> 00:42:51,720
by fully effective connections between the purlins

823
00:42:51,721 --> 00:42:55,800
and the brace to make sure that the arch can't move laterally.

824
00:42:55,801 --> 00:42:57,820
In the days that followed, a decision

825
00:42:57,821 --> 00:43:01,080
was made to rebuild the Rosemont Horizon following exactly

826
00:43:01,081 --> 00:43:05,460
the same design plans, but with a different contractor.

827
00:43:05,461 --> 00:43:09,040
When we erected the roof the second time,

828
00:43:09,041 --> 00:43:14,740
Each 24-foot section was completely installed

829
00:43:14,741 --> 00:43:18,440
and 100% complete before we moved on to the next section,

830
00:43:18,441 --> 00:43:23,680
which in essence allowed each one to be 100% secure

831
00:43:23,681 --> 00:43:26,760
before we moved the supporting structures.

832
00:43:26,761 --> 00:43:30,360
The Rosemont Horizon has been renamed the Allstate Arena,

833
00:43:30,361 --> 00:43:33,500
and it's served its community for more than 20 years.

834
00:43:33,501 --> 00:43:36,940
One design, two executions.

835
00:43:36,941 --> 00:43:39,040
The roof failure of the earlier arena

836
00:43:39,041 --> 00:43:41,820
demonstrates that avoiding an engineering disaster

837
00:43:41,821 --> 00:43:44,260
relies as much on the diligence of the builder

838
00:43:44,261 --> 00:43:46,020
as it does on the skill of the architect.

839
00:43:48,920 --> 00:43:53,100
Grain elevators, carrier ships, coal waste dams,

840
00:43:53,101 --> 00:43:55,080
and modern structures.

841
00:43:55,081 --> 00:44:00,120
In the 1970s, they were all catastrophes waiting to happen.

842
00:44:00,121 --> 00:44:02,260
And all provided hard-learned lessons

843
00:44:02,261 --> 00:44:04,280
that without proper safety regulations,

844
00:44:04,281 --> 00:44:07,160
vigilant public scrutiny, and professional diligence,

845
00:44:07,740 --> 00:44:11,600
an engineering feat can become an engineering disaster.

